/* Oral prep — theory modules. stars: 3 very likely, 2 likely, 1 possible. sec = section id for the theory link. */
OS.oral.analysis = `
<p>How the stars were assigned: <b>(1)</b> what the written quiz of this course already asked (it took definitions almost word for word from the slides: dispatcher, starvation, FCFS, waiting time, context switch and memory, MLFQ, PCS/SCS, race condition on pid, mutual exclusion, Peterson on modern CPUs, process memory sections, thread definition, the variable → mutex → semaphore → monitor order, L3 cache, cp -r, /root, /bin vs /sbin, /etc); <b>(2)</b> how many slides, examples and exercises a topic has; <b>(3)</b> recency — Main Memory and the i7 presentation are the newest material and were not in the quiz yet.</p>
<table class="t">
  <tr><th>Topic</th><th>Chance</th><th>Why</th></tr>
  <tr><td><b>CPU scheduling</b> (criteria, dispatcher, FCFS/SJF/RR/priority, MLFQ)</td><td>★★★</td><td>5 quiz questions; Gantt-chart examples are perfect oral material</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Synchronization</b> (race condition, critical section, Peterson, mutex, semaphores, monitors)</td><td>★★★</td><td>4 quiz questions; two lab sessions (ES4, ES7)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Processes</b> (definition, memory sections, states, PCB, context switch, fork)</td><td>★★★</td><td>core of the course; quiz asked sections and context switch</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Main memory</b> (paging, TLB + EAT, fragmentation, first/best/worst fit)</td><td>★★★</td><td>newest lecture (76 slides), not covered by the quiz — likely tested now</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Deadlock</b> (4 conditions, RAG, prevention, safe state, Banker's)</td><td>★★★</td><td>classic oral topic; easy to ask with a drawing</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Threads</b> (definition, vs process, models, Amdahl)</td><td>★★</td><td>quiz asked the thread definition</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Memory hierarchy, caching, i7 caches</b></td><td>★★</td><td>quiz asked about L3; links modules 1, 8 and 9</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Linux</b> (directories, permissions/chmod, commands, ps/kill/jobs)</td><td>★★</td><td>4 quiz questions on the file system</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>OS basics, system calls, OS structures</b></td><td>★★</td><td>typical opening question: "what is an OS / a system call?"</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>I/O</b> (programmed / interrupt / DMA) and <b>mass storage</b> (disk scheduling, RAID)</td><td>★★</td><td>clear comparison questions</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Bash</b>, <b>device management</b></td><td>★</td><td>more likely as "read this script / what does this command do"</td></tr>
</table>
<p><b>Practise drawing</b> before the exam: the process state diagram, a Gantt chart (FCFS vs SJF vs RR on the same processes), a resource-allocation graph with a cycle, and the paging translation (logical address → p, d → page table → frame).</p>`;
OS.addOral('basics', [
  { stars: 3, sec: 'what-is-os', q: 'What is an operating system?', a: 'A program that acts as an <b>intermediary between the user and the computer hardware</b>. Its goals: execute user programs and make solving problems easier, make the system convenient to use, and use the hardware efficiently. It is both a <b>resource allocator</b> (CPU, memory, I/O) and a <b>control program</b> (prevents errors and improper use). The <b>kernel</b> is the one program running at all times.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'interrupts', q: 'What is an interrupt and how is it handled?', a: 'A signal that makes the CPU stop what it is doing: a device controller raises it when it finishes an operation. The CPU <b>saves the state</b> (registers, PC), looks up the <b>interrupt vector</b> (table of addresses of the interrupt service routines), runs the ISR, then <b>restores</b> the state and resumes. A <b>trap/exception</b> is a software-generated interrupt (an error such as division by zero, or a system call). The OS is <b>interrupt-driven</b>.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'storage', q: 'Describe the storage hierarchy.', a: 'Registers → cache → main memory → SSD (nonvolatile memory) → hard disk → optical disk / tape. Going <b>down</b>: bigger, cheaper per byte, slower; going <b>up</b>: faster, smaller, more expensive. Registers, cache and main memory are <b>volatile</b>. Main memory is the only large storage the CPU accesses directly.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'caching', q: 'What is caching?', a: 'Copying information <b>in use</b> from slower to faster storage. The CPU checks the cache first: <b>hit</b> → use it directly (fast); <b>miss</b> → copy the data into the cache, then use it. Because the cache is smaller than what it caches, <b>cache management</b> (size and replacement policy) matters. Each level of the hierarchy acts as a cache for the level below.', more: ['Managed by: registers → compiler, cache → hardware, main memory and below → OS.'] },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'dual-mode', q: 'What is dual-mode operation? Why do we need a timer?', a: 'The hardware has a <b>mode bit</b>: <b>kernel mode (0)</b> and <b>user mode (1)</b>. <b>Privileged instructions</b> run only in kernel mode, so a user program cannot damage the OS or other programs. A <b>system call</b> switches to kernel mode (via a trap) and returns to user mode. The <b>timer</b> interrupts after a set period so the OS gets control back — it prevents a process from looping forever and holding the CPU.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'dma', q: 'What is DMA and why is it used?', a: '<b>Direct Memory Access</b>: for high-speed devices, the device controller transfers a <b>whole block</b> of data directly between its buffer and main memory <b>without the CPU</b>. Only <b>one interrupt per block</b> is generated instead of one per byte, so the CPU is free for other work.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'multiprogramming', q: 'Multiprogramming vs multitasking (timesharing)?', a: '<b>Multiprogramming</b> (batch): several jobs in memory; when one waits for I/O the OS switches to another, so the CPU is always busy. <b>Multitasking / timesharing</b> is its extension: the CPU switches so frequently that users can interact with each running program; response time should be <b>&lt; 1 second</b>.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'architecture', q: 'Single-processor vs multiprocessor systems (SMP, multicore, NUMA)?', a: '<b>Multiprocessor</b> (parallel) systems have several CPUs sharing memory and bus. Advantages: <b>increased throughput, economy of scale, increased reliability</b> (graceful degradation / fault tolerance). <b>Asymmetric</b>: each processor has a specific task; <b>SMP</b>: every processor performs all tasks. <b>Multicore</b>: several cores on one chip (faster on-chip communication, less power). <b>NUMA</b>: each CPU has local memory; access to remote memory is slower.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'clustered', q: 'What is a clustered system?', a: 'Several complete systems working together, usually sharing storage through a SAN, to provide <b>high availability</b>. <b>Asymmetric clustering</b>: one machine in hot-standby mode; <b>symmetric clustering</b>: several nodes run applications and monitor each other. Some clusters are for high-performance computing (applications must be parallelized).' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'startup', q: 'How does a computer start (bootstrap)?', a: 'At power-up the <b>bootstrap program</b>, stored in ROM/EEPROM (<b>firmware</b>), initializes the system and <b>loads the OS kernel</b> into memory and starts it. On Linux: BIOS/UEFI (POST) → boot loader (GRUB) → kernel → systemd (PID 1) → login.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'organization', q: 'Device controller vs device driver?', a: 'A <b>device controller</b> is <b>hardware</b> in charge of a device type, with a <b>local buffer</b> and registers. A <b>device driver</b> is <b>OS software</b> that understands the controller and offers the rest of the OS a <b>uniform interface</b>. CPU and controllers work concurrently; the controller tells the CPU it has finished by raising an interrupt.' },
  { stars: 1, sec: 'resource-mgmt', q: 'What does the OS manage? (process, memory, storage, I/O)', a: '<b>Process management</b>: create/delete, suspend/resume, synchronization, communication, deadlock handling. <b>Memory</b>: track which parts are used and by whom, allocate/deallocate, decide what to move in/out. <b>Storage / file system</b>: files, directories, free space, disk scheduling. <b>I/O subsystem</b>: buffering, caching, spooling, drivers.' },
  { stars: 1, sec: 'protection', q: 'Protection vs security?', a: '<b>Protection</b>: any mechanism controlling the <b>access</b> of processes or users to resources defined by the OS. <b>Security</b>: defence of the system against <b>internal and external attacks</b> (viruses, denial-of-service, identity theft…).' },
  { stars: 1, sec: 'virtualization', q: 'Virtualization vs emulation?', a: '<b>Virtualization</b> runs guest OSes on the same CPU type, under a <b>VMM</b> (hypervisor). <b>Emulation</b> is used when the source CPU type differs from the target (e.g. PowerPC → x86) — every instruction is translated, so it is slow.' },
  { stars: 1, sec: 'environments', q: 'Name the computing environments.', a: 'Traditional, mobile, <b>client–server</b>, <b>peer-to-peer</b> (no distinction between client and server), <b>cloud</b> (IaaS, PaaS, SaaS; public, private, hybrid), <b>real-time embedded</b> (fixed time constraints — processing must finish within them).' },
  { stars: 1, sec: 'kernel-ds', q: 'Which data structures does a kernel use?', a: 'Lists (singly, doubly, circular), stacks (LIFO), queues (FIFO), trees (binary search tree O(n), balanced O(lg n), red-black in Linux), <b>hash maps</b> (O(1) lookup), <b>bitmaps</b> (n bits for the status of n items).' }
]);

OS.addOral('services', [
  { stars: 3, sec: 'syscalls', q: 'What is a system call? How does it work?', a: 'The <b>programming interface to the services of the OS</b>. Programs normally use an <b>API</b> (POSIX, Windows API, Java API) that calls the system call for them. Each system call has a <b>number</b>; the <b>system-call interface</b> uses it as an index into a table, switches to <b>kernel mode</b> (trap), runs the routine and returns the status and results.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'params', q: 'How are parameters passed to a system call?', a: 'Three ways: (1) in <b>registers</b> (fast, but few); (2) in a <b>block / table in memory</b>, whose address is passed in a register (Linux and Solaris); (3) pushed on the <b>stack</b> by the program and popped by the OS. Block and stack do not limit the number or length of parameters.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'types', q: 'Types of system calls?', a: '<b>Process control</b> (create/terminate, load/execute, wait, allocate memory), <b>file management</b> (create, open, read, write, close), <b>device management</b> (request, release, read, write), <b>information maintenance</b> (time, date, attributes), <b>communications</b> (send/receive messages, shared memory), <b>protection</b> (permissions). Examples: fork / CreateProcess, open / CreateFile, read / ReadFile.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'monolithic', q: 'Compare the OS structures: monolithic, layered, microkernel, modules, hybrid.', a: '<b>Monolithic</b> (original UNIX, Linux): everything in one kernel address space — very fast, but hard to implement and extend. <b>Layered</b>: layer N uses only layers below — easy to debug, hard to define layers, some overhead. <b>Microkernel</b> (Mach): only minimal functions in the kernel, services in user space communicating by <b>message passing</b> — reliable, secure, extensible, but slower. <b>Modules</b>: loadable kernel modules (Linux). <b>Hybrid</b> (Windows, macOS): mixes these to get performance + modularity.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'api', q: 'Why use an API instead of calling system calls directly?', a: '<b>Portability</b> (the program runs on any system with the same API) and <b>simplicity</b> (system calls are more detailed and harder to use). The API hides the system-call details; the C library (libc) implements it, e.g. printf() calls write().' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'services', q: 'Which services does an OS provide?', a: 'For the <b>user</b>: user interface (CLI, GUI, touch), program execution, I/O operations, file-system manipulation, communications, error detection. For the <b>system itself</b>: resource allocation, logging (accounting), protection and security.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'linkers', q: 'What do the linker and the loader do?', a: 'Source code is compiled into <b>relocatable object files</b>; the <b>linker</b> combines them (and libraries) into an <b>executable</b>; the <b>loader</b> brings the executable into memory and starts it (relocation = assigning final addresses).' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'static-dynamic', q: 'Static vs dynamic linking?', a: '<b>Static</b>: library code is copied into the executable at link time — bigger file, no dependencies. <b>Dynamic</b>: libraries (DLL, .so) are linked at load/run time and shared by many programs — smaller files, one copy in memory, easy updates.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'design', q: 'Policy vs mechanism?', a: 'The <b>mechanism</b> says <b>how</b> to do something; the <b>policy</b> says <b>what</b> will be done. Separating them gives flexibility: the policy can change without rewriting the mechanism (e.g. a timer is a mechanism; how long the quantum is, is a policy).' },
  { stars: 1, sec: 'boot', q: 'How is an OS booted?', a: 'A small <b>bootstrap loader</b> in firmware locates the kernel, loads it into memory and starts it. Often two steps: firmware loads a <b>boot block</b>, which loads the boot loader (<b>GRUB</b>), which loads the kernel; then the kernel mounts the root file system and starts system services (systemd).' },
  { stars: 1, sec: 'debugging', q: 'How is an OS debugged / tuned?', a: 'Failure analysis with <b>log files</b>, <b>core dumps</b> (process memory) and <b>crash dumps</b> (kernel memory); performance tuning by removing bottlenecks using counters (top, vmstat) or <b>tracing</b> (strace, BCC/eBPF). Kernighan\'s idea: debugging is harder than writing the code.' },
  { stars: 1, sec: 'cli', q: 'CLI vs GUI?', a: 'The <b>command-line interpreter (shell)</b> reads a command and executes it — either built in or as a separate program (UNIX). The <b>GUI</b> uses a desktop metaphor with mouse, icons, windows; touchscreens use gestures.' }
]);

OS.addOral('processes', [
  { stars: 3, sec: 'concept', q: 'What is a process? How is it different from a program?', a: 'A process is a <b>program in execution</b>. A program is a <b>passive</b> entity (an executable file on disk); it becomes a process — an <b>active</b> entity — when it is loaded into memory. One program can be several processes. A process has a program counter, registers and its own memory.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'memory', q: 'What are the sections of a process in memory?', a: 'Four sections: <b>text</b> (the code), <b>data</b> (global variables, initialized and uninitialized), <b>heap</b> (memory allocated dynamically at run time, grows up), <b>stack</b> (temporary data: function parameters, return addresses, local variables; grows down).' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'states', q: 'Describe the process states.', a: '<b>New</b> (being created) → <b>ready</b> (waiting for the CPU) → <b>running</b> (executing) → <b>waiting</b> (waiting for I/O or an event) → back to ready; <b>terminated</b> when it finishes. Transitions: admitted, scheduler dispatch, interrupt (running → ready), I/O or event wait, I/O or event completion, exit.', more: ['Draw the diagram — it is almost always asked.'] },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'pcb', q: 'What is the PCB and what does it contain?', a: 'The <b>Process Control Block</b> is the kernel structure that represents a process: process state, program counter, CPU registers, CPU-scheduling information (priority, queue pointers), memory-management information, accounting information, I/O status (open files, devices). In Linux it is <b>task_struct</b>.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'context-switch', q: 'What is a context switch?', a: 'When the CPU switches to another process, the OS <b>saves the state</b> of the old process <b>in its PCB</b> and <b>loads the saved state</b> of the new one. It is <b>pure overhead</b> — no useful work is done — and its time depends on the hardware (e.g. multiple register sets). It also costs memory performance: <b>cache misses</b> and <b>TLB flushes</b> afterwards.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'fork', q: 'How is a process created in UNIX? (fork, exec, wait)', a: '<b>fork()</b> creates a child that is a <b>copy</b> of the parent; it returns <b>0 in the child</b> and the <b>child\'s PID in the parent</b> (negative on error). <b>exec()</b> replaces the process memory with a new program. The parent calls <b>wait()</b> to wait for the child to terminate. With n sequential forks there are <b>2ⁿ processes</b>.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'ipc', q: 'Shared memory vs message passing (IPC)?', a: 'Cooperating processes need <b>IPC</b>. <b>Shared memory</b>: a region shared by the processes; fast, no kernel after setup, but the processes must synchronize themselves. <b>Message passing</b>: send(message) / receive(message) through the kernel; easier, no conflicts, works across a network, but slower (system call per message).' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'producer-consumer', q: 'Explain the producer–consumer problem (bounded buffer).', a: 'A producer puts items into a buffer, a consumer takes them out. With a circular buffer and pointers <b>in</b> / <b>out</b>: empty when <code>in == out</code>, full when <code>(in + 1) % BUFFER_SIZE == out</code>, so at most <b>BUFFER_SIZE − 1</b> items. Using a shared <code>counter</code> instead allows all slots, but counter++ / counter-- cause a <b>race condition</b>.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'schedulers', q: 'Long-term vs short-term (vs medium-term) scheduler?', a: '<b>Short-term</b> (CPU scheduler): picks the next process to run; invoked very often (milliseconds), must be fast. <b>Long-term</b> (job scheduler): picks which processes enter the ready queue; invoked rarely; controls the <b>degree of multiprogramming</b> and the mix of I/O-bound and CPU-bound processes. <b>Medium-term</b>: swapping processes out and in.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'termination', q: 'Zombie vs orphan process?', a: 'A <b>zombie</b> has terminated but its parent has not yet called wait() — its PCB entry stays. An <b>orphan</b>\'s parent terminated without calling wait(); it is adopted by <b>init/systemd</b>. Some OSes use <b>cascading termination</b>: if the parent ends, all its children are terminated.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'race', q: 'Why is counter++ a race condition?', a: '<code>counter++</code> is three machine instructions (load into register, add, store). If the producer\'s and consumer\'s instructions interleave, e.g. both load 5, one stores 6 and the other 4, the final value is <b>4 or 6 instead of 5</b>. The result depends on the order of execution.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'message-passing', q: 'Message passing: direct vs indirect, blocking vs non-blocking, buffering?', a: '<b>Direct</b>: processes name each other (send(P, msg)); <b>indirect</b>: through <b>mailboxes (ports)</b>. <b>Blocking</b> (synchronous) send/receive wait; <b>non-blocking</b> return immediately (receive may get null). Both blocking = <b>rendezvous</b>. Buffering: zero capacity (sender waits), bounded (waits if full), unbounded (never waits).' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'pipes', q: 'Ordinary vs named pipes?', a: '<b>Ordinary pipe</b>: unidirectional, producer writes to the <b>write end fd[1]</b>, consumer reads from the <b>read end fd[0]</b>; needs a <b>parent–child</b> relationship. <b>Named pipe (FIFO)</b>: bidirectional, no parent–child relationship needed, several processes can use it, exists in the file system.' },
  { stars: 1, sec: 'sockets', q: 'What is a socket?', a: 'An <b>endpoint for communication</b>, identified by <b>IP address + port</b> (e.g. 161.25.19.8:1625). Communication happens between a pair of sockets. Ports below 1024 are well known (standard services); <b>127.0.0.1</b> is loopback.' },
  { stars: 1, sec: 'posix-shm', q: 'POSIX shared memory: which calls?', a: '<b>shm_open()</b> creates/opens the object, <b>ftruncate()</b> sets its size, <b>mmap()</b> maps it into the address space (MAP_SHARED: changes visible to all); then read/write it like memory; shm_unlink() removes it.' },
  { stars: 1, sec: 'android-chrome', q: 'Why does Chrome use several processes?', a: 'Isolation: a <b>browser</b> process (UI, disk, network), one <b>renderer</b> process per website running in a <b>sandbox</b>, and <b>plug-in</b> processes. If one site crashes, only its renderer dies.' }
]);

OS.addOral('threads', [
  { stars: 3, sec: 'definition', q: 'What is a thread?', a: 'The <b>smallest unit of CPU execution within a process</b>. It has its own thread ID, program counter, registers and <b>stack</b>; it <b>shares</b> with the other threads of the process the code, data, open files and signals.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'benefits', q: 'Why use threads? (benefits)', a: '<b>Responsiveness</b> (the program keeps working while part of it is blocked), <b>resource sharing</b> (memory shared by default), <b>economy</b> (creating and switching threads is cheaper than processes), <b>scalability</b> (threads run in parallel on multicore).' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'vs-process', q: 'Process vs thread?', a: 'A process is heavy-weight: own address space, creation and context switch are expensive, communication needs IPC, a crash does not affect other processes. Threads are light-weight: they share the address space, are cheap to create and switch, communicate through shared memory, but a crash of one thread kills the whole process.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'multicore', q: 'Concurrency vs parallelism?', a: '<b>Concurrency</b>: more than one task makes progress (on one core, by interleaving). <b>Parallelism</b>: tasks really run <b>at the same time</b> on several cores. You can have concurrency without parallelism. Types of parallelism: <b>data</b> (same operation on parts of the data) and <b>task</b> (different operations on cores).' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'amdahl', q: 'State Amdahl\'s law.', a: 'speedup ≤ <b>1 / (S + (1 − S) / N)</b>, S = serial fraction, N = cores. Example: 75% parallel, 25% serial, 1 → 2 cores gives <b>1.6</b>; 4 cores ≈ 2.28. As N → ∞ the speedup tends to <b>1 / S</b>: the serial part limits the gain.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'models', q: 'User threads vs kernel threads; the multithreading models?', a: '<b>User threads</b> are managed by a library; <b>kernel threads</b> by the OS. Mapping: <b>many-to-one</b> (one blocking call blocks all, no parallelism), <b>one-to-one</b> (Windows, Linux: more concurrency, but many kernel threads), <b>many-to-many</b> (multiplexed, flexible, rare), <b>two-level</b> (M:M plus binding a user thread to a kernel thread).' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'pthreads', q: 'How do you create and wait for a thread with Pthreads?', a: '<code>pthread_create(&tid, &attr, function, arg)</code> starts a thread running <code>function(arg)</code>; <code>pthread_join(tid, NULL)</code> waits for it to finish; <code>pthread_exit()</code> ends it. Include pthread.h and link with -lpthread.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'args', q: 'What is wrong with passing &t to pthread_create in a loop?', a: 'All threads receive the <b>address of the same variable t</b>; by the time they read it, the loop has changed it, so they print wrong/duplicated ids. Fix: pass the address of a <b>separate element per thread</b> (e.g. an array taskids[t]).' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'cancellation', q: 'Thread cancellation: asynchronous vs deferred?', a: '<b>Asynchronous</b>: the target thread is terminated immediately (risky — it may hold resources or be updating data). <b>Deferred</b> (default): the thread checks periodically and terminates only at a <b>cancellation point</b> (e.g. pthread_testcancel()), then a cleanup handler runs.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'fork-exec', q: 'Threading issues: fork/exec and signals?', a: 'fork() in a threaded program: some UNIXes duplicate <b>only the calling thread</b>, others <b>all threads</b>; if exec() follows immediately, duplicating only the caller is enough. Signals: deliver to the thread the signal applies to, to every thread, to some threads, or to one assigned thread.' },
  { stars: 1, sec: 'tls', q: 'What is thread-local storage?', a: 'Data that each thread has its <b>own copy</b> of (unlike global data), but visible across function calls (unlike local variables) — e.g. a per-thread transaction ID.' },
  { stars: 1, sec: 'sched-act', q: 'What are LWPs and scheduler activations?', a: 'An <b>LWP</b> (lightweight process) is an intermediate structure between user and kernel threads — a "virtual processor" for the library. <b>Scheduler activations</b>: the kernel informs the library with <b>upcalls</b> (e.g. a thread is about to block) so it can adjust the number of LWPs.' },
  { stars: 1, sec: 'linux', q: 'How does Linux implement threads?', a: 'Linux calls them <b>tasks</b> and creates them with <b>clone()</b>; flags (CLONE_FS, CLONE_VM, CLONE_SIGHAND, CLONE_FILES) decide what is shared with the parent. No flags = like fork; all flags = a thread.' }
]);

OS.addOral('scheduling', [
  { stars: 3, sec: 'dispatcher', q: 'Scheduler vs dispatcher? What is dispatch latency?', a: 'The <b>CPU scheduler</b> selects a process from the ready queue. The <b>dispatcher</b> gives it the CPU: <b>context switch, switch to user mode, jump to the right location</b> in the program. <b>Dispatch latency</b> = time for the dispatcher to stop one process and start another.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'criteria', q: 'What are the scheduling criteria?', a: 'Maximize <b>CPU utilization</b> and <b>throughput</b> (processes completed per time unit); minimize <b>turnaround time</b> (arrival → completion), <b>waiting time</b> (time spent <b>in the ready queue</b>) and <b>response time</b> (submission → first response).', more: ['Turnaround = completion − arrival; waiting = turnaround − burst.'] },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'preemption', q: 'Preemptive vs non-preemptive scheduling?', a: 'Decisions happen when a process (1) running → waiting, (2) running → ready, (3) waiting → ready, (4) terminates. Only in 1 and 4 → <b>non-preemptive</b>: a process keeps the CPU until it blocks or ends (FCFS, SJF). Also in 2 and 3 → <b>preemptive</b>: the OS can take the CPU away (RR, SRTF) — better response, but overhead, starvation and race conditions. Modern OSes are preemptive (Linux CFS).' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'fcfs', q: 'FCFS — how does it work and what is the convoy effect?', a: '<b>First-Come, First-Served</b>: FIFO ready queue, <b>non-preemptive</b>. Simple but the average waiting time depends on arrival order: P1=24, P2=3, P3=3 → average <b>17</b>; order P2, P3, P1 → <b>3</b>. <b>Convoy effect</b>: short processes wait behind one long process.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'sjf', q: 'SJF and SRTF?', a: '<b>SJF</b> runs the process with the shortest <b>next CPU burst</b>; it is <b>optimal</b> (minimum average waiting time) but the next burst is unknown — it must be estimated or asked. Example 8, 4, 9, 5 → average <b>7.5</b>. <b>SRTF</b> is preemptive SJF: on every arrival, run the shortest <b>remaining</b> time (slide example → <b>6.5</b>).' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'rr', q: 'Round Robin?', a: 'Each process gets a <b>time quantum q</b> (10–100 ms); when it expires a timer interrupt puts it at the end of the ready queue. With n processes no one waits more than <b>(n − 1)·q</b>. q too large → behaves like <b>FCFS</b>; q too small → too many context switches (q must be large compared with the switch time, &lt; 10 µs). Higher turnaround than SJF but better response.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'priority', q: 'Priority scheduling, starvation and aging?', a: 'The CPU goes to the highest priority (<b>smallest integer</b>); preemptive or non-preemptive. SJF is priority with priority = inverse of the predicted burst. Problem: <b>starvation</b> (indefinite blocking) — a <b>ready</b> low-priority process may never run. Solution: <b>aging</b> — increase the priority of processes that wait a long time.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'mlfq', q: 'Multilevel queue vs multilevel feedback queue?', a: '<b>MLQ</b>: several ready queues (e.g. real-time, system, interactive, batch), each with its own algorithm; a process <b>stays</b> in its queue. <b>MLFQ</b>: a process <b>can move</b> between queues — demoted if it uses too much CPU, upgraded if it waits too long (aging). Example: Q0 RR 8 ms → Q1 RR 16 ms → Q2 FCFS.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'threads', q: 'Thread scheduling: PCS vs SCS?', a: 'In many-to-one and many-to-many, the library schedules user threads on <b>LWPs</b>: competition is <b>within the process</b> → <b>Process-Contention Scope (PCS)</b>. In one-to-one the kernel schedules kernel threads among <b>all threads in the system</b> → <b>System-Contention Scope (SCS)</b>. Linux and macOS allow only PTHREAD_SCOPE_SYSTEM.' },
  { stars: 1, sec: 'multicore', q: 'What is chip multithreading (hyperthreading)?', a: 'Each core has several <b>hardware threads</b>; when one stalls on memory, the core runs another. A 4-core CPU with 2 threads per core shows <b>8 logical processors</b>. Two levels of scheduling: the OS picks software threads for logical CPUs, the core picks which hardware thread runs.' }
]);

OS.addOral('sync', [
  { stars: 3, sec: 'race', q: 'What is a race condition?', a: 'Several processes access and manipulate <b>shared data concurrently</b> and the result depends on the <b>order</b> of execution. Slide example: two processes call fork() at the same time and read <code>next_available_pid</code> before it is incremented — the <b>same pid could be assigned to two different processes</b>.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'requirements', q: 'What is the critical-section problem and its three requirements?', a: 'Each process has a <b>critical section</b> where it changes shared data; the problem is to design a protocol (entry section / exit section) so that: <b>1. Mutual exclusion</b> — if Pi is in its critical section, <b>no other process</b> can be in theirs; <b>2. Progress</b> — if nobody is inside, the choice of who enters next cannot be postponed indefinitely; <b>3. Bounded waiting</b> — a limit on how many times others can enter before a waiting process does.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'peterson', q: 'Explain Peterson\'s solution.', a: 'A software solution for <b>two processes</b> with shared <code>int turn</code> and <code>boolean flag[2]</code>. Pi sets <code>flag[i] = true; turn = j;</code> then waits <code>while (flag[j] &amp;&amp; turn == j)</code>; after the critical section <code>flag[i] = false</code>. It satisfies mutual exclusion, progress and bounded waiting.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'reorder', q: 'Does Peterson\'s solution work on modern architectures?', a: '<b>No, not guaranteed</b>: to improve performance, processors and compilers may <b>reorder</b> independent read/write operations. For multithreaded code the reordering can produce inconsistent or unexpected results (both processes in the critical section). Fix: <b>memory barriers</b> (or hardware atomic instructions).' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'mutex', q: 'What is a mutex lock?', a: 'The simplest tool: a boolean <code>available</code> protected by atomic <b>acquire()</b> and <b>release()</b>. A process acquires the lock before the critical section and releases it after. Its drawback is <b>busy waiting</b>: a waiting process loops on acquire() — hence the name <b>spinlock</b> (fine only for short waits on multiprocessors).' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'semaphore', q: 'What is a semaphore?', a: 'An integer S accessed only through atomic <b>wait()</b> (P: wait while S ≤ 0, then S--) and <b>signal()</b> (V: S++). <b>Binary</b> (0/1, like a mutex) or <b>counting</b> (N resources). Uses: mutual exclusion (init 1), limiting access to N resources (init N), ordering (init 0: S1 then signal, other waits). More sophisticated than a mutex.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'sem-impl', q: 'How is a semaphore implemented without busy waiting?', a: 'Each semaphore has a <b>value</b> and a <b>list of waiting processes</b>. wait(): value--; if value &lt; 0, add the process to the list and <b>block()</b>. signal(): value++; if value ≤ 0, remove a process from the list and <b>wakeup()</b> it. A negative value = number of waiting processes.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'monitors', q: 'What is a monitor? What are condition variables?', a: 'A <b>high-level abstraction</b> (ADT): shared data + procedures + initialization; <b>only one process at a time</b> can be active inside — the compiler adds the mutual exclusion. <b>Condition variables</b> (<code>condition x</code>) let a process wait inside: <b>x.wait()</b> suspends it, <b>x.signal()</b> resumes one waiting process (none → no effect). Options: signal-and-wait or signal-and-continue.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'sem-problems', q: 'Problems with semaphores?', a: 'Easy to misuse: signal() before wait() (mutual exclusion violated), wait() twice (deadlock), omitting wait() or signal(). Incorrect use can cause <b>deadlock</b> and <b>starvation</b>. Monitors were introduced to avoid these errors.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'rw', q: 'Readers–writers problem?', a: 'Many readers may read together; a writer needs exclusive access. First solution: semaphores <b>rw_mutex = 1</b> (writers / first and last reader), <b>mutex = 1</b> (protects read_count), int <b>read_count = 0</b>. The first reader locks rw_mutex, the last one releases it. Writers may <b>starve</b>.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'philosophers', q: 'Dining philosophers?', a: '5 philosophers, 5 chopsticks (semaphores). Taking the left then the right chopstick can <b>deadlock</b> if all take the left one at once. Remedies: at most 4 at the table; take both only if both are free; asymmetric order (odd: left first, even: right first). The <b>monitor</b> solution (states thinking/hungry/eating, test(i)) avoids deadlock but starvation is still possible.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'printers', q: 'The 3-printers exercise: which semaphores?', a: 'A <b>counting semaphore initialized to 3</b> limits how many processes print at the same time; a <b>binary semaphore (mutex) = 1</b> protects the choice of <b>which</b> printer is free, so two processes never take the same printer.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'summary', q: 'Order the synchronization tools from lowest to highest level.', a: '<b>Shared variable → mutex lock → semaphore → monitor</b>. Each one fixes a weakness of the previous (busy waiting, misuse); none prevents deadlock by itself.' },
  { stars: 1, sec: 'interrupts', q: 'Why not just disable interrupts?', a: 'It works on a single CPU (no preemption inside the critical section), but not on <b>multiprocessors</b>, long critical sections freeze the system, and some processes could starve.' }
]);

OS.addOral('deadlock', [
  { stars: 3, sec: 'model', q: 'What is a deadlock?', a: 'A situation where two or more processes (threads) wait <b>forever</b> for an event (releasing a resource) that only another waiting process can cause. System model: a thread <b>requests</b>, <b>uses</b> and <b>releases</b> resources.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'conditions', q: 'What are the four necessary conditions for deadlock?', a: 'All four must hold at the same time: <b>1. Mutual exclusion</b> (a resource used by one at a time), <b>2. Hold and wait</b> (holding one, waiting for another), <b>3. No preemption</b> (released only voluntarily), <b>4. Circular wait</b> (T0 waits for T1, …, Tn waits for T0).' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'rag', q: 'What is a resource-allocation graph? How does it show deadlock?', a: 'Vertices: threads (circles) and resource types (rectangles with dots = instances). <b>Request edge</b> T → R, <b>assignment edge</b> R → T. <b>No cycle → no deadlock</b>. <b>Cycle</b>: with <b>one instance</b> per resource → <b>deadlock</b>; with several instances → <b>possibility</b> of deadlock.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'methods', q: 'How can an OS handle deadlocks?', a: 'Three ways: <b>prevention or avoidance</b> (never enter deadlock), <b>detection and recovery</b> (allow it, then fix it), or <b>ignore</b> the problem (most OSes, including Linux and Windows).' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'prevention', q: 'How does deadlock prevention work?', a: 'Make one of the four conditions impossible. Mutual exclusion: not needed for sharable resources (cannot remove for non-sharable). Hold and wait: request all resources at start or only when holding none (low utilization, starvation). No preemption: take resources from a waiting process. <b>Circular wait</b> (most common): impose a <b>total ordering</b> of resources and request them in increasing order.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'safe-state', q: 'What is a safe state?', a: 'A state is <b>safe</b> if there is a <b>safe sequence</b> &lt;T1…Tn&gt; in which each Ti\'s remaining needs can be satisfied by the available resources plus those held by all Tj before it. Safe → <b>no deadlock</b>; unsafe → <b>possibility</b> of deadlock (not certain). Avoidance = never leave a safe state.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'banker', q: 'Explain the Banker\'s algorithm.', a: 'Avoidance for <b>multiple instances</b>: each thread declares its <b>maximum</b> need. Data: <b>Available</b>, <b>Max</b>, <b>Allocation</b>, <b>Need = Max − Allocation</b>. <b>Safety algorithm</b>: Work = Available; repeatedly find Ti with Need ≤ Work, then Work += Allocation_i; if all finish → safe. <b>Request</b>: check Request ≤ Need and ≤ Available, pretend to allocate, run safety: safe → grant, unsafe → wait.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'rag-avoid', q: 'Avoidance with a single instance per resource?', a: 'Use the RAG with <b>claim edges</b> (dashed T → R: may request in the future). A request is granted only if converting the claim edge into an assignment edge does <b>not create a cycle</b>.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'detection', q: 'How is deadlock detected?', a: '<b>Single instance</b>: build the <b>wait-for graph</b> (collapse resource nodes; Ti → Tj if Ti waits for Tj) and search for a <b>cycle</b> (O(n²)). <b>Multiple instances</b>: an algorithm like the safety algorithm using Available, Allocation, Request; threads that cannot finish are deadlocked. How often to run it depends on how often deadlocks occur.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'recovery', q: 'How do we recover from deadlock?', a: '<b>Process termination</b>: abort all deadlocked processes, or one at a time until the cycle disappears (choose by priority, run time, resources held…). <b>Resource preemption</b>: select a <b>victim</b> (minimize cost), <b>rollback</b> it to a safe state, avoid <b>starvation</b> (include the number of rollbacks in the cost).' }
]);

OS.addOral('memory', [
  { stars: 3, sec: 'base-limit', q: 'How does the hardware protect memory? (base and limit registers)', a: 'Each process gets a range of legal addresses defined by a <b>base register</b> (smallest address) and a <b>limit register</b> (size). The CPU checks every user-mode access: <b>base ≤ address &lt; base + limit</b>, otherwise a <b>trap</b> to the OS. Only the OS can load these registers (privileged instructions).' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'binding', q: 'What is address binding? When can it happen?', a: 'Mapping program addresses to memory addresses: symbolic (variable names) → <b>relocatable</b> (by the compiler) → <b>absolute</b> (by the linker/loader). At <b>compile time</b> (absolute code if the location is known), <b>load time</b> (relocatable code), or <b>execution time</b> (the process can move during execution — needs hardware support, the MMU).' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'logical-physical', q: 'Logical vs physical address? What is the MMU?', a: 'The CPU generates a <b>logical (virtual) address</b>; the memory unit sees a <b>physical address</b>. They differ with execution-time binding. The <b>MMU</b> is the hardware that maps logical to physical at run time; in the simple scheme the <b>relocation register</b> is added to every address (e.g. 14000 + 346 = 14346). The program never sees physical addresses.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'allocation', q: 'Contiguous allocation and first-, best-, worst-fit?', a: 'Each process gets one contiguous block (variable partitions sized to the process). A request of size n is satisfied from the list of free <b>holes</b>: <b>first-fit</b> = the first hole big enough; <b>best-fit</b> = the smallest big enough (smallest leftover); <b>worst-fit</b> = the largest (largest leftover). First- and best-fit are better than worst-fit in speed and storage use.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'fragmentation', q: 'External vs internal fragmentation?', a: '<b>External</b>: enough total free memory exists but it is <b>not contiguous</b> (variable partitions) — fixed by <b>compaction</b> (needs execution-time binding) or by paging. <b>Internal</b>: allocated memory slightly <b>larger than requested</b>; the unused part is inside the partition (fixed partitions, the last page). <b>50-percent rule</b>: with first-fit, N allocated blocks lose 0.5 N to fragmentation (1/3 unusable).' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'paging', q: 'What is paging and how is an address translated?', a: 'Physical memory is divided into fixed-size <b>frames</b>, logical memory into <b>pages</b> of the same size; a <b>page table</b> per process gives the frame of each page — the process need not be contiguous, so <b>no external fragmentation</b>. The logical address is split into <b>page number p</b> (index into the page table) and <b>offset d</b>: physical = frame(p) × page size + d. With 2^m addresses and page size 2^n: p = high m − n bits, d = low n bits.', more: ['Slide example: page size 4, page table [5, 6, 1, 2]: logical 13 → p = 3, d = 1 → frame 2 → physical 9.'] },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'tlb', q: 'What is the TLB? Compute the effective access time.', a: 'The page table is in memory (PTBR points to it), so every access needs <b>two memory accesses</b>. The <b>TLB (translation look-aside buffer)</b> is a small, fast <b>associative memory</b> caching recent page → frame translations. <b>EAT = α × m + (1 − α) × 2m</b>: 80% hit, 10 ns → <b>12 ns</b> (20% slowdown); 99% → <b>10.1 ns</b>. Some TLBs store <b>ASIDs</b> so they need not be flushed at every context switch.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'internal-frag', q: 'Compute internal fragmentation with paging.', a: 'Page size 2,048 bytes, process 72,766 bytes = 35 pages + 1,086 bytes → 36 frames, internal fragmentation <b>2,048 − 1,086 = 962 bytes</b>. Worst case: 1 frame − 1 byte; average: half a frame. Small pages → less fragmentation but bigger page tables.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'protection', q: 'How does paging protect memory? (valid–invalid bit, shared pages)', a: 'Each page-table entry has <b>protection bits</b> (read-only / read-write / execute) and a <b>valid–invalid bit</b>: valid = the page is in the process\'s logical address space; invalid → trap. <b>Shared pages</b>: one copy of <b>reentrant</b> (read-only) code, e.g. a library, mapped into the page tables of many processes.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'hierarchical', q: 'Why are page tables so big and how do we structure them?', a: '32-bit addresses with 4 KB pages → 2^20 entries (4 MB per process). Solutions: <b>hierarchical (two-level) paging</b> — page the page table: 10 | 10 | 12 bits (p1 outer index, p2 inner index, d offset; forward-mapped); <b>hashed page tables</b> (for &gt; 32-bit spaces; chains of virtual page → frame); <b>inverted page table</b> — one entry per physical frame for the whole system (less memory, slower search, hard to share).' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'swapping', q: 'What is swapping?', a: 'A process can be moved temporarily out of memory to a <b>backing store</b> and brought back later, so the total memory of all processes can exceed physical memory. Moving whole processes is slow (context-switch time grows with process size), so modern systems swap <b>pages</b> (swapping with paging). Mobile systems usually do not swap (flash wear, little space).' },
  { stars: 1, sec: 'loading', q: 'Static vs dynamic loading / linking?', a: '<b>Dynamic loading</b>: a routine is loaded only when it is called — better memory use, no special OS support. <b>Dynamic linking</b>: linking postponed to execution time (shared libraries); a <b>stub</b> locates the library routine.' },
  { stars: 1, sec: 'intel', q: 'How does Intel IA-32 translate addresses?', a: 'It supports <b>segmentation and segmentation with paging</b>: the CPU generates a logical address, the <b>segmentation unit</b> turns it into a <b>linear address</b>, the <b>paging unit</b> turns that into a physical address (two-level paging 10 | 10 | 12, or 4 MB pages). PAE allows more than 4 GB of physical memory; x86-64 uses 48-bit virtual addresses with four levels.' }
]);

OS.addOral('i7', [
  { stars: 3, sec: 'caching-basics', q: 'Describe the cache hierarchy of the Intel Core i7.', a: '<b>Three levels</b>. Each core has its own <b>L1</b> (split into <b>instruction</b> and <b>data</b> halves) and its own <b>L2</b>. The <b>L3</b> is <b>shared by all cores</b> and <b>inclusive</b> (it also holds what is in L1/L2); it is on the CPU <b>die</b>, outside the individual cores. Example i7-4790: L1 4 × 32 KB 8-way, L2 4 × 256 KB 8-way, L3 shared 8 MB 16-way.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'access-steps', q: 'What happens on a memory access in the i7? (latencies)', a: 'Check <b>L1</b>: hit ≈ <b>4 cycles</b>. Miss → <b>L2</b> ≈ <b>10 cycles</b>. Miss → <b>L3</b> ≈ <b>35 cycles</b>. Miss → <b>main memory</b> ≈ <b>135 cycles</b>; the block is then placed in L1 and L3. Each level is a cache for the next one.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'tlb', q: 'How is the TLB organized in the i7?', a: 'Two levels: <b>L1 TLB</b> split into data TLB (64 entries, 4 KB pages) and instruction TLB (128 entries); <b>L2 TLB</b> (services L1 DTLB misses): 1024 entries, 4 KB and 2 MB pages, 8-way (previous generation: 512 entries, 4-way).' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'associativity', q: 'What is N-way set associativity?', a: 'The cache is divided into <b>sets</b>; a memory block can go only into one set, but into any of its <b>N ways</b> (slots). It narrows the search to one set while reducing conflicts. More ways = fewer conflict misses but more comparisons.' },
  { stars: 1, sec: 'basic-structure', q: 'Basic structure of the i7?', a: '<b>4 cores</b>, <b>hyper-threaded → 8 threads</b>, a <b>16-stage pipeline</b>. Generations: Nehalem (2008), Sandy Bridge (2011), Ivy Bridge (2012), Haswell (2013), Broadwell and Skylake (2015).' },
  { stars: 1, sec: 'plru', q: 'Which replacement algorithm does the i7 cache use?', a: '<b>Pseudo-LRU</b>: one bit per cache line, set when the line is used; when all bits are set they are reset; the victim is the <b>lowest-index line with bit 0</b>.' },
  { stars: 1, sec: 'prefetch', q: 'What are prefetching and TSX?', a: '<b>Prefetching</b>: fetch instructions/data before they are needed — on a miss two blocks are fetched. <b>TSX</b> (transactional synchronization extensions): hardware lock elision and restricted transactional memory, to run critical sections without locking when there is no conflict.' }
]);

OS.addOral('io', [
  { stars: 3, sec: 'modes', q: 'Compare programmed I/O, interrupt-driven I/O and DMA.', a: '<b>Programmed I/O</b>: the CPU drives everything and <b>busy-waits</b> on the status register — simple but wastes the CPU. <b>Interrupt-driven</b>: the CPU starts the operation and does other work; the device raises an <b>interrupt</b> when ready — but one interrupt per word is still costly for fast devices. <b>DMA</b>: the DMA controller moves the <b>whole block</b> between device and memory and interrupts <b>once at the end</b>; the CPU only supervises.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'polling', q: 'What is polling (busy waiting)?', a: 'The CPU repeatedly reads the device <b>status register</b> until the device is ready (or checks several devices in turn). It wastes CPU cycles while waiting; acceptable only if the device is fast.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'devices', q: 'What makes up an I/O device? Which registers does a controller have?', a: 'An I/O device = the <b>physical device</b> + its <b>controller</b> (electronic part). The controller has registers the CPU reads/writes: <b>status</b> (busy, ready, error), <b>control</b> (command), <b>data-in</b> and <b>data-out</b>. The driver talks to these registers.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'interrupts', q: 'Interrupts vs exceptions; maskable vs non-maskable?', a: '<b>Interrupts</b> come from outside the CPU (devices, timer); <b>exceptions</b> come from the program: <b>trap</b> (intentional, e.g. system call), <b>fault</b> (recoverable, e.g. page fault), <b>abort</b> (unrecoverable). <b>Non-maskable</b> interrupts are for serious errors and cannot be disabled; <b>maskable</b> ones can be turned off during critical sequences. Interrupts have <b>priorities</b>.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'drivers', q: 'What is a device driver?', a: 'OS software (often a kernel module) that knows a specific controller and hides its details behind a <b>standard interface</b> (open, read, write, ioctl…), so the rest of the OS treats different devices uniformly.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'buffering', q: 'Buffering vs caching (vs spooling)?', a: '<b>Buffer</b>: memory holding data <b>while it is transferred</b> (copes with speed or size differences); may be the only copy. <b>Cache</b>: a faster <b>copy</b> of data stored elsewhere, for performance. <b>Spooling</b>: a buffer holding output for a device that cannot interleave jobs (a printer).' },
  { stars: 1, sec: 'bus', q: 'What is a bus? Which bus types exist?', a: 'The communication channel among components: <b>data, address and control lines</b>. Types: processor–memory bus (short, fast, often proprietary), I/O bus (long, standard), backplane bus.' }
]);

OS.addOral('storage', [
  { stars: 3, sec: 'hdd-perf', q: 'How is the access time of a hard disk computed?', a: '<b>Access time = average seek time</b> (move the arm to the cylinder) + <b>average rotational latency</b> (half a rotation = ½ × 60/RPM) + transfer time + controller overhead. Slide example: 4 KB, 7200 RPM, 5 ms seek, 1 Gb/s, 0.1 ms → 5 + 4.17 + 0.1 + 0.03 ≈ <b>9.3 ms</b>.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'fcfs', q: 'Disk scheduling algorithms?', a: 'Goal: minimize <b>seek time</b> (head movement). <b>FCFS</b> (fair, 640 cylinders in the slide example), <b>SSTF</b> (closest request first, 236, may starve requests), <b>SCAN</b> (elevator: end to end), <b>LOOK</b> (turns at the last request, 208), <b>C-SCAN</b> / <b>C-LOOK</b> (serve in one direction, jump back — more uniform waiting). SCAN/C-SCAN are better for heavy load.', more: ['Queue 98, 183, 37, 122, 14, 124, 65, 67, head 53.'] },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'raid', q: 'What is RAID? Mirroring vs striping?', a: '<b>Redundant Array of Independent Disks</b>: many disks used together for <b>reliability</b> (redundancy) and/or <b>performance</b>. <b>Mirroring</b> (RAID 1): every disk duplicated — reliable, reads faster, cost doubles. <b>Striping</b> (RAID 0): data split across disks — throughput grows with N, no redundancy.' },
  { stars: 3, sec: 'raid-levels', q: 'Describe the main RAID levels.', a: '<b>RAID 0</b> striping; <b>RAID 1</b> mirroring; <b>RAID 4</b> block striping + a dedicated parity disk (bottleneck on writes); <b>RAID 5</b> parity <b>distributed</b> over all disks (most common, survives 1 failure); <b>RAID 6</b> P + Q (survives 2); <b>RAID 0+1</b> mirror of stripes; <b>RAID 1+0</b> stripe of mirrors (more robust).' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'ecc', q: 'How does parity recover a failed disk?', a: 'Parity = <b>XOR</b> of the data blocks. If one disk fails, XOR of the remaining disks and the parity rebuilds its content. <b>ECC</b> (error-correcting codes) store extra bits to detect and also correct errors.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'nand', q: 'SSD / NAND flash: advantages and problems?', a: 'No moving parts → <b>no seek or rotational latency</b>, faster and more reliable, but more expensive per MB and shorter life. NAND cannot <b>overwrite</b> in place: pages are written, blocks must be <b>erased</b> first; blocks wear out. The controller uses the <b>FTL</b> (maps logical to valid physical pages), <b>garbage collection</b>, <b>over-provisioning</b> and <b>wear leveling</b>.' },
  { stars: 2, sec: 'attachment', q: 'Host-attached vs NAS vs SAN vs cloud storage?', a: '<b>Host-attached</b>: local ports (SATA, USB). <b>NAS</b>: storage over the network accessed as <b>files</b> through RPC (NFS, SMB) over TCP/UDP. <b>SAN</b>: a private network (Fibre Channel) giving <b>block</b> access to storage arrays. <b>Cloud storage</b>: over the Internet/WAN through an API.' },
  { stars: 1, sec: 'hdd', q: 'Structure of a hard disk?', a: '<b>Platters</b> covered with magnetic material, divided into <b>tracks</b> and <b>sectors</b>; the tracks at the same position on all platters form a <b>cylinder</b>; one <b>read-write head</b> per surface on a moving arm.' }
]);
